The prologue barrier.

The retail keeps the stack update first and the mflr second, and it saves
the link register deeper in the body.  Three mechanisms were measured;
they are written up in docs/ABI.md.  This patch publishes the barrier
that reproduces that order.  The option is off by default.

gen_blockage is type integer.  On CELL a true dependence from
cell-integer to cell-load costs 5 cycles, with no guard, so a load stays
queued while the store of the link register becomes ready.  The pattern
below is not that type.  It is an unspec_volatile the recognizer does not
match as "blockage".  Its type is mtcr: one cycle, on fxu_sim_cell, and
not a producer of that bypass.  The template is empty, and the length is
0 so branch shortening does not count a byte that is not emitted.

With -mps3-prologue-barrier the mflr is grouped with the stack update,
the barrier is emitted immediately after that pair, and the store of the
link register uses rs6000_sr_alias_set, the set the epilogue already uses
to reload the slot.  Off, none of that happens.

Measured with the option on, on a function that adds one to its argument
and passes a global:

    stdu 1,-112(1)
    mflr 5
    lwz  9,.LC0@toc(2)
    addi 3,3,1
    std  5,128(1)

In the sched2 log the barrier is issued at cycle 2 on fxu_sim_cell, the
TOC load and the addi together at cycle 3, and the store at cycle 4.
The load is not queued for 5 cycles.

The three files this patch touches - gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.c,
gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.md and gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.opt - are
upstream GNU Compiler Collection, and each carries the Free Software
Foundation's notice:

    This file is part of GCC.  GCC is free software; you can redistribute
    it and/or modify it under the terms of the GNU General Public License
    as published by the Free Software Foundation; either version 2, or (at
    your option) any later version.

A modification of a GPL file is under the GPL, so this patch is published
under the same terms, with the notices kept.  It touches no file that Sony
added to GCC, and quotes no SDK header, library or sample.  See
patches/README.md for what it does.  Apply it after ps3-lr-reg.patch,
ps3-toc-neg-disp.patch and ps3-neg-disp-completion.patch.
diff --git a/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.c b/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.c
index 8c8f594..5b36b31 100644
--- a/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.c
+++ b/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.c
@@ -15854,6 +15854,17 @@ rs6000_emit_prologue (void)
       insn = emit_move_insn (gen_rtx_REG (Pmode, ps3_lr_reg),
 			     gen_rtx_REG (Pmode, LINK_REGISTER_REGNUM));
       RTX_FRAME_RELATED_P (insn) = 1;
+      /* Group the mflr with the stack update, then stop the scheduler
+	 from moving anything else above that pair.  gen_blockage is type
+	 integer, and on CELL a true dependence from cell-integer to a load
+	 costs 5 cycles, so the load would stay queued while the store of
+	 the link register became ready.  This barrier is not that type.
+	 The save itself stays after it.  Off by default.  */
+      if (ps3_prologue_barrier)
+	{
+	  SCHED_GROUP_P (insn) = 1;
+	  emit_insn (gen_ps3_prologue_block ());
+	}
     }
 
   /* Call a function to save GPRs.  Sometimes this needs r12 so do it
@@ -16114,8 +16125,14 @@ rs6000_emit_prologue (void)
 			       GEN_INT (offset));
       rtx reg = gen_rtx_REG (Pmode, ps3_lr_reg);
       rtx mem = gen_rtx_MEM (Pmode, addr);
-      /* This should not be of rs6000_sr_alias_set, because of
-	 __builtin_return_address.  */
+      /* Alias set 0 conflicts with every load, so the store cannot
+	 sink past a load whose address is not a known distinct base.  The
+	 epilogue already reads the slot with rs6000_sr_alias_set.  The
+	 option gives the store that set; off, the set stays 0.  A count of
+	 zero for __builtin_return_address reads the hard register, and a
+	 higher count uses alias set 0, which still conflicts with this.  */
+      if (ps3_prologue_barrier)
+	set_mem_alias_set (mem, rs6000_sr_alias_set);
 
       insn = emit_move_insn (mem, reg);
       rs6000_frame_related (insn, frame_ptr_rtx, info->total_size,
diff --git a/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.md b/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.md
index 79d3c69..1c2260f 100644
--- a/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.md
+++ b/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.md
@@ -11427,6 +11427,19 @@
       DONE;
 }")
 
+;; A scheduling barrier that is not type integer.  gen_blockage is, and
+;; on CELL a true dependence from cell-integer to cell-load costs 5
+;; cycles, so a load stays queued while a store becomes ready.  Type mtcr
+;; has a one-cycle reservation and is not a producer of that bypass.  The
+;; template is empty, and the length is 0 so branch shortening does not
+;; count a byte that is not emitted.
+(define_insn "ps3_prologue_block"
+  [(unspec_volatile [(const_int 1)] UNSPECV_BLOCK)]
+  ""
+  ""
+  [(set_attr "type" "mtcr")
+   (set_attr "length" "0")])
+
 ;; UNSPEC_VOLATILE is considered to use and clobber all hard registers and
 ;; all of memory.  This blocks insns from being moved across this point.
 
diff --git a/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.opt b/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.opt
index f20e7f0..9966830 100644
--- a/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.opt
+++ b/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000.opt
@@ -325,3 +325,11 @@ Read the entry of the TOC in one instruction, from r2.
 mps3-neg-disp
 Target Var(ps3_neg_disp) Init(0)
 Fold a negative displacement into the load.
+
+; Do not schedule a body instruction before the stack update, and give
+; the save of the link register the alias set of the epilogue, so the
+; store can sink into the body.  Off by default: the functions matched
+; without the option must keep matching.
+mps3-prologue-barrier
+Target Var(ps3_prologue_barrier) Init(0)
+Do not schedule instructions before the stack update.
